    "recommendations": "Reducing the density of shrub cover and brush in the immediate vicinity of the structure could meaningfully improve defensible space and lower fuel continuity around the building. Creating and maintaining separation between shrubs and the structure's exterior walls, foundation, and any attached features such as decks or fencing would support suppression operations and reduce direct flame contact opportunities. Managing vegetation within the tree cluster and reducing surface fuel connections between scattered shrubs and trees may reduce the opportunity for fire to move through the property as a continuous fuel bed. Along the driveway, clearing low-hanging branches and reducing brush encroachment on both sides could improve firefighter access and support safe evacuation, particularly given the length of the route. Maintaining a fuel-reduced corridor along the full driveway length would support two-way passage for emergency vehicles and improve overall access reliability. Ongoing vegetation management within the area closest to the structure should be prioritized to support long-term wildfire resilience."
